{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-22703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2{e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-22703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x-2{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2{e}^{x} & >0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/c199b110a3d19ecc23454f52dc7470af318ce871805595fd5fe7609dee5b0545.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that ${e}^{x}<1$ when $x<0.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${e}^{x}>1,$ which has the solution $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-22703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80124d00ddd3a04cecb7a4ac6d768dd31c50b7af2a468040e6925bb75e5538c3"} {"question" "q-22703" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-22703" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-22703" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-22809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-22809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ over the interval $(0,\\pi ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0$ when $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ which has the solution $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-22809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80124d00ddd3a04cecb7a4ac6d768dd31c50b7af2a468040e6925bb75e5538c3"} {"question" "q-22809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-22809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are correct"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-22809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-114061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/d3bd98f5662140655dd2bd33f99dc8704b1bd03b6004133dc8492ac51ea3a347.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={h}^{″}(x).$  Find the intervals on which the function $h(x)$ is concave down." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-114061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $h(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${h}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={h}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${h}^{″}(x)<0$ on the intervals $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,4).$  Therefore, the function $h(x)$ is concave down on the intervals $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-114061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80124d00ddd3a04cecb7a4ac6d768dd31c50b7af2a468040e6925bb75e5538c3"} {"question" "q-114061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-114061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-4,4)"] ["math" "(-\\infty ,-4)"] ["math" "(4,\\infty )"] ["text" "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,4)$"] ["text" "$(-\\infty ,-4),$ $(-2,2),$ and $(4,\\infty )$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-114061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,4)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-114259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/a7330541d6102141aec87f6733d09585553de672c5e55d2f84fe70196e4c6b5f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x),$ where the function $f(x)$ is defined on $[-2,2].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-114259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(-1,1).$ Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval  $(-1,1)$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-114259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80124d00ddd3a04cecb7a4ac6d768dd31c50b7af2a468040e6925bb75e5538c3"} {"question" "q-114259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-114259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,1)"] ["math" "(-2,-1)"] ["math" "(-2,2)"] ["text" "$(-2,-1)$ and $(1,2)$"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-114259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-1,1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-344783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/345b36f79184e0857bfc63693b2be0a968ba308035af9548136c5b7ba692ab52.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-5,$ $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $x=4,$ and $x=6,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,-5),\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$),\\,(${{field-4}}$,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d3b85c4d46b5eb946a5b79c8882ee1e2aa5c7f8e28123462f5705ec88ad3d41"} {"question" "q-344783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-1,1),$ and $(4,6).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the intervals \n\n$$\n(\\,-\\infty ,-5\\,),\\,(\\,-1,1\\,),\\,(\\,4,6\\,).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/referenc